\fs20 Figure 2 shows the contents of the first block of the System Programs directory.  Each program entry occupies six words:\par
\par
\pard\tx947\tx1042 Word 1-3: The program name in six-bit text format.  All trailing characters of\par
\tab   program names shorter than six characters are nul [00].  Any character\par
\tab   that is valid for a \i Tiny File System\i0  [TFS] file name is also valid for a system\par
\tab   program name.  This includes characters such as \i\\\i0 , \i ^\i0 , \i [\i0  and \i ]\i0 .\par
\pard\par
Word 4:   The starting block number for the program.  The contents of this block (and any \tab     relevant additional contiguous blocks) will be loaded into memory when an\par
\tab     execution-class keyboard monitor command initiates the program.\par
\par
\tab     Note: Since system programs in the basic P?S/8 system are loaded by calls to \tab     the system device handler, the starting block number is a 12-bit unsigned \tab     value.  When the optional P?S/8 \i SHELL\i0  overlay is implemented, programs will be\par
\tab     controlled by independent entries in the SHELL filesystem directory.  AS such,\par
\tab     the starting block will be expressed as a 24-bit unsigned integer which will be \tab     passed to a designated non-system device handler.\par
\par
\tab     Note: The additional words in each entry are likely to change in the next \tab     release; this may include changing the exact count of words per entry from six \tab     to seven.  Due to the generous allocation of blocks in the system programs \tab     directory, this is not expected to be of any practical consequence.\page Word 5:   This word contains several sets of loading and parsing information:\par
\par
\tab     Bits[0-4] contain the loading address of the system program (which must\par
\tab     conform to full page boundaries).\par
\par
\tab     Bits[7-11] contain the length (in 128-word blocks) of the initial loading\par
\tab     segment of the program.\par
\par
\tab     Note: All programs that do not utilize overlay structures contain no additional\par
\tab     segments.  Programs that include overlay mechanisms define their own internal\par
\tab     structures as required.  For example, P?S/8 \i FOCAL\i0  includes several secondary\par
\tab     segments that are loaded into memory by FOCAL in specific circumstances; P?S/8\par
\tab     \i PAL\i0  has an analogous set of secondary segments of program code and a data\par
\tab     segment that consists of the internal symbol table that is loaded into memory\par
\tab     as determined by dynamic memory allocation considerations.\par
\par
\tab     If Bit[5] is set, the command processor will not scan for (input or\par
\tab     output) filenames.  Certain programs are designed to process the rest of\par
\tab     the command line directly.  For example, the P?S/8 \i CORE\i0  program sets this\par
\tab     bit to allow processing of various in-line arguments that must not be\par
\tab     confused with filenames.\par
\par
\tab     If Bit[6] is set, processing of an \i =xxxx\i0  expression will occur in the\par
\tab     current radix setting within the keyboard monitor.  Otherwise, the\par
\tab     expression is always processed as octal notation.  For example, the P?S/8\par
\tab     \i FIND\i0  program locates strings within file lines (within the edit buffer of\par
\tab     the keyboard monitor) matching a specified string.  The search can be\par
\tab     limited to a starting line number expressed as \i =xxxx\i0  in the current radix\par
\tab     setting within the keyboard monitor.\par
\par
Word 6:   This word contains several sets of startup and parsing parameters:\par
\par
\tab     Bits[0-4] contains the startup address for the program.  Note: As of\par
\tab     P?S/8 Version 8Z, all system programs must start on full page boundaries.\par
\tab     Future releases may relax this restriction somewhat.\par
\par
\tab     If Bit[5] is set, the parsing of input files is inhibited.  For example,\par
\tab     the P?S/8 \i BSAVE\i0  program must be passed output files without input files.\par
\tab     After the input/output separator character (\i <\i0  or \i >\i0  according to user\par
\tab     preference) has been processed, the list of output files is always\par
\tab     processed first.  All command-line input beyond the separator character\par
\tab     must be available to the BSAVE program for direct processing.  This\par
\tab     section of the command consists of individual memory locations or groups\par
\tab     of contiguous memory locations to be saved in the specified binary output\par
\tab     files; however, if input file processing were not inhibited, certain\par
\tab     memory location specifications could be mistaken for input files.\par
\par
\tab     Bits[6-11] are used for passive cosmetic purposes only.  Various\par
\tab     combinations of potential input and output file types have been defined.\par
\tab     The P?S/8 \i DIRECT\i0  program output as shown in Figure 1 includes the\par
\tab     designated values for all programs that are ordinarily used with input\par
\tab     files or output files (or both).\par
\par
\tab     Future P?S/8 releases will drop support of the usage of Bits[6-11] in Word 6 as\par
\tab     described above.  These bits will be repurposed to allow the program starting \tab     address to be any value within field 0.  This will be implemented by moving the \tab     special scan bits to a seventh word; this will also allow for future system \tab     expansion such as theoretical SHELL compatibility allowing the SHELL \tab     subsystem to access compatible programs from the System Programs directory.\par
\par
\tab     Note: SHELL-based executable files will support a starting address anywhere \tab     within the entire 32K PDP-8 memory space.\page\fs12\par
