\par
\fs20 Figure 3 shows the contents of the second block of the System Programs directory.  Several empty entries exist in this block indicating there is ample room for additional system programs.\par
\par
Note: To add a significant amount of additional system programs, the system device must be much larger than a DECtape or other relatively small storage media.  Larger devices can address up to block 7777 which would allow significant additional image program support.\par
\par
P?S/8 execution is initiated by one of two methods: concise command execution (starting the command with the program name), or by use of the R (or RU\{N\}) command specifying the desired program by name.\par
\par
Note: In the current release, the RU\{N\} command is an alias for the R command; either can be used interchangeably.\par
\par
When the R (or RU\{n\}) command method is used, the entire directory is available for system programs; in this mode of usage, a total of 125 octal (85 decimal) system programs can be accommodated.  Figure 1 indicates 072 octal (58 decimal) slots are available as the System Programs directory currently contains 33 octal (27 decimal) programs.\par
\par
When concise command execution is used, a restriction is made to only access entries wholly contained in the first two blocks of the System Programs directory.  (Note: Any entry past relative word[173] of the second System Programs directory block is destroyed in memory by the command processor.)  As such, when concise command execution is used, the maximum number of accessible slots is reduced to 52 octal (42 decimal).  With regard to the System Programs directory used in the example, 17 octal [15 decimal] programs can still be added while also avoiding the possibility of being unavailable for the concise execution method.\par
\par
Note: The two methods of initiating program execution have several tradeoffs:\par
\par
1) Certain programs are implemented as overlays to the keyboard monitor such as the \i FIND\i0  program used to locate string matches within the tentative file edit buffer of the keyboard monitor.  Only the concise execution method preserves the contents of the edit buffer; attempting to use FIND via the method of R FIND would be moot as the edit buffer contents will be destroyed.\par
\par
2) Certain programs such as P?S?8 \i OS8CON\i0  can be executed to make use of the extended user directory for the purpose of creating variable-length files.  The concise execution form cannot access the extended-length file directory.\par
\par
The general recommendation is to place programs with little interaction with P?S/8 resources in higher blocks to ensure programs implemented as overlays to always be executable by the concise method.  These generally include device formatting or diagnostic programs designed for standalone usage (with the paper-tape loading programs \i RIM\i0  or \i BIN\i0 .)\par
\par
Note: The support of extended-length files is undergoing major redesign.  As originally intended, it was foreseen that P?S/8 might attempt to include certain aspects of variable-length files in a more general-purpose way than merely one extended-length file per system device logical unit; however, no version of P?S/8 ever supported more than read access to the first file and the file name had to be manually entered into the first entry in the extended-length files directory.\page With the implementation of the P?S/8 SHELL (currently under development) the extended-length files directory is being eliminated in favor of a single entry placed at the end of the System Programs directory.  As such, the maximum number of system programs available to the R (or RU\{n\}) command will be reduced from 125 octal (85 decimal) to 123 octal (83 decimal).  Since the entry for the single extended-length file will be loaded into memory along with the rest of the System Programs directory, overall command efficiency will be notably improved.\par
\par
Appropriate modifications will be made to the P?S/8 \i SET\i0  command to accommodate the single extended-length file; it will no longer be necessary to enter the file name manually.\par
\par
Due to this design change, the R (or RU\{n\}) form of execution cannot access the extended-length directory presently.  A workaround is available as follows:\par
\par
The TFS directory manages all blocks up to the limit block just before the extended-length file directory area.  As such, a TFS file entry can be placed at the last allowed file slot.  This file entry will start exactly 20 octal (16 decimal) blocks before the (first and only) extended-length file.  As such, this final TFS directory slot can be used in lieu of an extended-length file name as necessary.\par
\par
Note: The file will not be compatible with the keyboard monitor edit-oriented commands. All system programs can be passed this file in the same manner an extended-length file would ordinarily be used.\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 OS8CON and related programs have been altered to not check validity of the passed file block; when access to the extended-length single file entry is restored, OS8CON validity checking will be restored.\par
\par
