\fs20 Figure 5 shows the contents of the fourth and final system programs directory block.  Relative word[177] contains the first available block for additional system programs.  All released P?S/8 systems provide a small number of spare blocks to allow additional programs to be added.  There are no utilities available to aid in the creation of additional system programs; advanced users are familiar enough with P?S/8 as documented within this document to successfully add additional programs.  In this particular example, blocks 0735-0777 are available for additional system programs.\page Note: The TFS directory for this system is the documented example in another document detailing P?S/8 TFS directories.  The TFS files for this system start in block 1000.\par
\par
Temporary system programs can be created by use of the R (or RU\{n\}) % or R (or\par
RU\{n\}) $ command.  When binary files are loaded and saved to the system save area (using the \i GET\i0  command or equivalent), it is possible to run the image as if in the System Programs directory via this command, complete with all commmand-line options; where relevant, the radix for the \i =xxxx\i0  is forced to octal.\par
\par
The only serious restriction on such usage is the inability to define overlay structures such as are used in P?S?8 PAL and P?S?8 FOCAL; proper implementation requires additional knowledge of P?S/8 internal operations.  Additionally, the program must start at location 00200 (which is the most likely address).  More information on this form of program execution is available in the \ul\i P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  document available separately.\par
