\fs20\par
Tentative definition of the extended-length file entry (for P?S/8 Version 9A).\line\line As a practical matter, the ending words of the fourth system programs directory block are always unused.  Since the full definition of the system programs directory is only used by the R (or RU\{n\}) form of execution, this is ideal placement for the extended-length file entry (as the command execution processor will have already loaded the block into memory).\par
\par
The tentative definitions are as follows:\line\line Relative words[162-171] are reserved for the scratch file allocation list.  This is primarily used by P?S/8 PAL as temporary storage of the cross-reference data if the /X option is in effect.  The references are linearly created during the second pass of the assembly; once the main assembly ends, the references are read back in and stored in memory in sorted order for printout after the last linear reference is processed.  Note: Multiple passes of the reference data may be necessary on very large assemblies.\par
\line Eight words are allocated to allow all possible logical units to provide the extended-length file entries.  For any system, the entries consist of the preferred order of the logical units desired.  All entries to be allocated will also have the high-order bit on available logical units (expressed in the three low-order bits).  During the reference dump process, the actual logical units will be read to determine available output blocks for each unit.  Note: Differing amounts will tend to be available depending on system configuration.\line\line Relative words[172-174 are reserved for the extended-length file name.  Note: This name must be unique with respect to the TFS directory on the same logical unit; if there is an anomaly, the TFS entry will always take preference.\line\line Relative word[175] is the starting block for the extended-length file.\line\line Relative word[176] is the high limit value for the extended length area on this logical unit.\line\line Note: All extended-length file entry information is optional; on certain systems, extended-length files may not be present.\par
